<!-- NEW CONCEPT: LAMBDA FUNCTIONS -->
<section class="oop-section">
    <div class="oop-section-header">
        <div class="oop-section-icon">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb"></i>
        </div>
        <h2 class="oop-section-title">New Concept: Lambda Functions</h2>
    </div>
    
    <div class="concept-explanation" style="background: #f8f9fa; border-left: 4px solid var(--primary-color); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
        <p style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">A <strong>lambda</strong> is a tiny, one-line function you write inline. The syntax is:</p>
        
        <div style="background: #1a1a2e; color: #f8f9fa; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px; font-family: 'Fira Code', monospace; font-size: 0.9rem; margin: 1rem 0;">
            <div><span style="color: #c9a227;">lambda</span> <span style="color: #6c757d;">parameter</span>: <span style="color: #6c757d;">expression</span></div>
        </div>
        
        <p><strong>Example:</strong> A lambda that doubles a number:</p>
        <div style="background: #1a1a2e; color: #f8f9fa; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px; font-family: 'Fira Code', monospace; font-size: 0.9rem; margin: 0.5rem 0;">
            <div>double = <span style="color: #c9a227;">lambda</span> n: n * 2</div>
            <div style="color: #6c757d; margin-top: 0.5rem;"># double(5) returns 10</div>
        </div>
        
        <p style="margin-top: 1rem;"><strong>Why use lambdas?</strong> Python's <code>sorted()</code> function has a <code>key</code> parameter that accepts a function. This function tells sorted() <em>what value</em> to compare.</p>
        
        <div style="background: #1a1a2e; color: #f8f9fa; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px; font-family: 'Fira Code', monospace; font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: 1rem;">
            <div style="color: #6c757d;"># Sort words by their LENGTH (not alphabetically)</div>
            <div>words = ["cat", "elephant", "dog"]</div>
            <div style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">sorted(words, key=<span style="color: #c9a227;">lambda</span> w: len(w))</div>
            <div style="color: #6c757d; margin-top: 0.5rem;"># Result: ["cat", "dog", "elephant"]</div>
            <div style="color: #6c757d;"># (3 letters, 3 letters, 8 letters)</div>
        </div>
        
        <p style="margin-top: 1rem;"><strong>Think about:</strong> If you have a list of dictionaries, how would you tell <code>sorted()</code> which dictionary key to sort by?</p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- CHALLENGE SPECIFICATIONS -->
<section class="oop-section">
    <div class="oop-section-header">
        <div class="oop-section-icon">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-code"></i>
        </div>
        <h2 class="oop-section-title">Function Specifications</h2>
    </div>
    
    <p class="section-intro">Implement these functions in <code>student/log_search.py</code>. Each log is a dictionary with keys like <code>'author'</code>, <code>'timestamp'</code>, <code>'message'</code>.</p>
    
    <!-- search_logs -->
    <details class="skeleton-block" open>
        <summary class="skeleton-summary">
            <span class="skeleton-step">1</span>
            <span class="skeleton-title">search_logs(logs, author)</span>
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down skeleton-chevron"></i>
        </summary>
        <div class="skeleton-content">
            <div class="challenge-spec" style="border: 1px solid #dee2e6; border-radius: 8px; overflow: hidden;">
                <div style="background: var(--primary-color); color: white; padding: 0.75rem 1rem; font-weight: 600;">
                    Filter logs by author
                </div>
                <div style="padding: 1rem;">
                    <p><strong>Parameters:</strong></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>logs</code> — a list of log dictionaries</li>
                        <li><code>author</code> — the author name to search for (string)</li>
                    </ul>
                    
                    <p style="margin-top: 1rem;"><strong>Returns:</strong> A new list containing only logs where the author matches.</p>
                    
                    <div style="background: #f8f9fa; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 1rem;">
                        <strong>Example:</strong>
                        <pre style="margin: 0.5rem 0; font-size: 0.85rem;">logs = [
    {"author": "Dr. Chen", "message": "Test 1"},
    {"author": "Dr. Smith", "message": "Test 2"},
    {"author": "Dr. Chen", "message": "Test 3"}
]
search_logs(logs, "Dr. Chen")
# Returns: [{"author": "Dr. Chen", ...}, {"author": "Dr. Chen", ...}]</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </details>
    
    <!-- sort_logs -->
    <details class="skeleton-block">
        <summary class="skeleton-summary">
            <span class="skeleton-step">2</span>
            <span class="skeleton-title">sort_logs(logs, key, descending)</span>
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down skeleton-chevron"></i>
        </summary>
        <div class="skeleton-content">
            <div class="challenge-spec" style="border: 1px solid #dee2e6; border-radius: 8px; overflow: hidden;">
                <div style="background: var(--primary-color); color: white; padding: 0.75rem 1rem; font-weight: 600;">
                    Sort logs by any field
                </div>
                <div style="padding: 1rem;">
                    <p><strong>Parameters:</strong></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>logs</code> — a list of log dictionaries</li>
                        <li><code>key</code> — which field to sort by (e.g., "timestamp", "author")</li>
                        <li><code>descending</code> — True for newest first, False for oldest first</li>
                    </ul>
                    
                    <p style="margin-top: 1rem;"><strong>Returns:</strong> A new sorted list of logs.</p>
                    
                    <p style="margin-top: 1rem;"><strong>Hint:</strong> Use Python's <code>sorted()</code> function. It takes two useful parameters:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>key</code> — a function that extracts the value to sort by</li>
                        <li><code>reverse</code> — True for descending order</li>
                    </ul>
                    
                    <div style="background: #f8f9fa; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 1rem;">
                        <strong>Example:</strong>
                        <pre style="margin: 0.5rem 0; font-size: 0.85rem;">logs = [
    {"timestamp": 3, "message": "Third"},
    {"timestamp": 1, "message": "First"},
    {"timestamp": 2, "message": "Second"}
]
sort_logs(logs, "timestamp", False)
# Returns logs ordered: timestamp 1, 2, 3

sort_logs(logs, "timestamp", True)
# Returns logs ordered: timestamp 3, 2, 1</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </details>

    <!-- validate_code -->
    <details class="skeleton-block">
        <summary class="skeleton-summary">
            <span class="skeleton-step">3</span>
            <span class="skeleton-title">validate_code(code, used_codes)</span>
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down skeleton-chevron"></i>
        </summary>
        <div class="skeleton-content">
            <div class="challenge-spec" style="border: 1px solid #dee2e6; border-radius: 8px; overflow: hidden;">
                <div style="background: var(--primary-color); color: white; padding: 0.75rem 1rem; font-weight: 600;">
                    Check if a code has been used
                </div>
                <div style="padding: 1rem;">
                    <p><strong>Parameters:</strong></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>code</code> — the code to check (string)</li>
                        <li><code>used_codes</code> — a set of codes that have already been used</li>
                    </ul>
                    
                    <p style="margin-top: 1rem;"><strong>Returns:</strong></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>True</code> if the code is valid (NOT in used_codes)</li>
                        <li><code>False</code> if the code is invalid (already used)</li>
                    </ul>
                    
                    <div style="background: #f8f9fa; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 1rem;">
                        <strong>Example:</strong>
                        <pre style="margin: 0.5rem 0; font-size: 0.85rem;">used = {"ABC123", "XYZ789"}

validate_code("ABC123", used)  # Returns: False (already used)
validate_code("NEW456", used)  # Returns: True (not used yet)</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </details>
</section>

